KNOWNBUG libcxx
main.cpp
--cpp11 --stdlib libc++ --unwind 6 --no-unwinding-assertions --object-bits 12
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ moved string keeps its contents: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ element: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
dropped the body
--
libc++ 18's basic_string(basic_string &&) initializes `__r_' through an
immediately-invoked lambda `[](basic_string &__s) -> decltype(__s.__r_) &&
{ ...; return std::move(__s.__r_); }(__str)' (<string> line 925).  The
lambda's body is dropped ("invalid implicit conversion from 'struct
__compressed_pair &' to 'struct __compressed_pair &&'"), so a moved-from
std::string is nondet under libc++ -- the moved string's size and elements
read as garbage.  Preceded by "symbol '__compressed_pair_elem' does not
uniquely resolve" in __compressed_pair's implicit copy constructor (two
private bases that are specializations of one template); the header-free
shape of that ambiguity alone (compressed_pair_elem<T1,0> and <T2,1> bases,
a lambda returning `decltype(s.r_) &&') converts correctly, so the full
libc++ context is needed to reproduce.  Under libstdc++ the same program
passes.  Audit residue item 1 (round 162), 11 libc++ tests.
